1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,431
In a crash, what separates accounts
isn't whether you feel fear,

2
00:00:03,431 --> 00:00:04,801
but whether you have a plan.

3
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:09,892
Mr. Jeong, too, kept cash to buy in a
crash and even wrote down a buying plan.

4
00:00:10,272 --> 00:00:13,875
But when the crash actually came, he
followed fear instead of the plan.

5
00:00:14,255 --> 00:00:15,613
As a result, 4 years later,

6
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:18,279
his 100 million won was down
to just over 35 million.

7
00:00:18,659 --> 00:00:21,103
Meanwhile, the index
recovered from that crash

8
00:00:21,103 --> 00:00:23,493
and rose more than 40%
over the same 4 years.

9
00:00:23,873 --> 00:00:26,872
Facing fear, Mr. Jeong
made five decisions;

10
00:00:26,872 --> 00:00:29,103
choose what you would have done.

11
00:00:29,483 --> 00:00:32,438
Just count how many of your
decisions gave in to fear.

12
00:00:32,818 --> 00:00:36,834
Now put yourself in Mr. Jeong's
shoes on the day fear first struck.

13
00:00:37,834 --> 00:00:41,114
You have 100 million won that you
won't need for more than 5 years.

14
00:00:41,495 --> 00:00:46,633
On the day the index hit an all-time high,
you put 60 million won into the S&P 500.

15
00:00:47,013 --> 00:00:51,053
You kept the other 40 million won
in cash, to buy if a crash came.

16
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:54,890
You also wrote a buying plan: how
much to buy at each level of decline.

17
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:59,929
Two months later, news of war pushed
the index more than 12% below its high.

18
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:02,717
The index showing how much
US stocks are expected

19
00:01:02,717 --> 00:01:04,781
to swing is called the fear index, or VIX.

20
00:01:05,161 --> 00:01:09,329
That fear index shot up to 36,
nearly double its usual level.

21
00:01:09,709 --> 00:01:12,535
Your 60 million won has
shrunk to about 52 million,

22
00:01:12,535 --> 00:01:14,807
and the news predicts only further falls.

23
00:01:15,187 --> 00:01:18,812
Right now, will you sell
everything before you lose more,

24
00:01:18,812 --> 00:01:20,910
and buy back once the fear fades?

25
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:24,989
Or will you hold, and take out
the buying plan you wrote down?

26
00:01:25,369 --> 00:01:26,309
Make your choice.

27
00:01:26,689 --> 00:01:28,869
3, 2, 1.

28
00:01:29,249 --> 00:01:31,487
Here, the right choice is not to sell,

29
00:01:31,487 --> 00:01:34,256
but to take out the buying
plan you wrote down.

30
00:01:34,636 --> 00:01:40,011
That day was March 7, 2022, eleven
days after Russia invaded Ukraine.

31
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:41,992
Suppose you, like Mr. Jeong,

32
00:01:41,992 --> 00:01:45,766
sold everything and bought back
when the fear index fell below 20.

33
00:01:46,146 --> 00:01:48,151
By then the index had risen 9%,

34
00:01:48,151 --> 00:01:51,514
so the same money would
have bought 8% fewer shares.

35
00:01:51,894 --> 00:01:56,125
So even by the end of 2025,
you'd have gained only 58%.

36
00:01:56,505 --> 00:02:00,315
Had you not sold, you'd have
been up 72% at the same point.

37
00:02:00,695 --> 00:02:04,515
Of course, selling that day would
have dodged the next drop for a while.

38
00:02:04,895 --> 00:02:09,274
But by the time the fear feels over,
prices have usually already risen.

39
00:02:09,654 --> 00:02:12,733
Since 1990, the fear index
has topped 35 seventeen times,

40
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:14,732
and that happened in sixteen of them.

41
00:02:15,112 --> 00:02:16,940
Here's an example of a plan.

42
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,926
Each time the index is 10%, 15%,

43
00:02:19,926 --> 00:02:24,323
20% and 25% below its high,
buy with 1/4 of your cash.

44
00:02:24,703 --> 00:02:26,872
So the right choice: the bigger the fear,

45
00:02:26,872 --> 00:02:29,623
don't sell; take out the
buying plan you wrote down.

46
00:02:30,623 --> 00:02:34,500
3 weeks later, the fear index
dropped below 20, its usual level.

47
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,708
Meanwhile, the index
had rebounded nearly 9%.

48
00:02:38,088 --> 00:02:42,488
Forums fill with posts showing that people
who bought on debt already made big gains.

49
00:02:42,868 --> 00:02:47,059
Your brokerage app pops up a notice:
you can borrow on margin to buy more.

50
00:02:47,439 --> 00:02:49,579
Right now, will you buy without debt,

51
00:02:49,579 --> 00:02:52,355
only with the money you
set aside for investing?

52
00:02:52,735 --> 00:02:56,311
Or will you borrow on margin and buy more?

53
00:02:56,691 --> 00:02:57,631
Make your choice.

54
00:02:58,011 --> 00:03:00,191
3, 2, 1.

55
00:03:00,571 --> 00:03:05,421
Here, the right choice is to buy without
debt, only with the money you set aside.

56
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:07,306
Suppose that, like Mr. Jeong,

57
00:03:07,306 --> 00:03:11,407
you bought back on March 28 and borrowed
the same amount on margin to buy more.

58
00:03:11,787 --> 00:03:13,705
When the index then fell 22%,

59
00:03:13,705 --> 00:03:17,276
your leveraged money would
have shrunk by nearly half.

60
00:03:17,656 --> 00:03:21,663
Without debt, you'd have
lost 21% at that point.

61
00:03:22,043 --> 00:03:25,903
Your stocks' value relative to the
loan would also have dropped to 151%.

62
00:03:26,283 --> 00:03:29,633
Just above the 140% line where the
broker force-sells your stocks.

63
00:03:30,013 --> 00:03:33,653
Of course, as long as the index only
rises, borrowing lets you earn more.

64
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:36,606
But debt doubles your
losses when prices fall,

65
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:38,900
and interest is charged every single day.

66
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,280
In the four bear markets since 1990,

67
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:44,890
buying the index with debt on day
one led to a forced sale twice.

68
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:48,168
So the right choice is to buy
only with money you set aside,

69
00:03:48,168 --> 00:03:50,825
keeping the strength to hold
on if prices fall further.

70
00:03:51,825 --> 00:03:56,134
6 weeks later, the index fell
back to 17% below its high.

71
00:03:56,514 --> 00:03:59,338
At that point, a drugmaker
made famous by its

72
00:03:59,338 --> 00:04:02,412
COVID vaccine had fallen
72% in less than 1 year.

73
00:04:02,792 --> 00:04:04,866
People say it'll never
be this cheap again,

74
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:07,036
and that buying now will
multiply your money.

75
00:04:07,416 --> 00:04:11,910
Right now, will you skip this stock
and keep your money in the index?

76
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:16,120
Or will you sell part of your
index holdings to buy this stock?

77
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:17,440
Make your choice.

78
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:20,000
3, 2, 1.

79
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:25,400
Here, the right choice is to skip this
stock and keep your money in the index.

80
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:29,759
That day was May 9, 2022,
and the stock was Moderna.

81
00:04:30,139 --> 00:04:33,321
If, like Mr. Jeong, you had sold
half your index to buy Moderna,

82
00:04:33,321 --> 00:04:35,856
it would briefly have
rebounded 55% late that year.

83
00:04:36,236 --> 00:04:40,456
But by the end of 2025, it would
be 78% below that day's price.

84
00:04:40,836 --> 00:04:42,625
Had you stayed in the index,

85
00:04:42,625 --> 00:04:46,394
you'd have been down only 10%
at worst, and up 81% by then.

86
00:04:46,774 --> 00:04:48,686
Of course, some stocks, like Netflix,

87
00:04:48,686 --> 00:04:51,734
were deeply down that day and
then rose more than fivefold.

88
00:04:52,114 --> 00:04:53,881
But among the hardest-hit stocks,

89
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:56,773
there was no way to know
that day which would recover.

90
00:04:57,153 --> 00:05:02,337
Take the 24 current S&P 500 companies
that were down the most that day.

91
00:05:02,717 --> 00:05:08,163
Within the next 2 years, 15 of them fell
more than 30% further at some point.

92
00:05:08,543 --> 00:05:11,989
So the right choice is to stick with
an index that holds many stocks,

93
00:05:11,989 --> 00:05:13,537
not one stock that looks cheap.

94
00:05:14,537 --> 00:05:18,707
Three months later, the index had
rallied 17% from its earlier low.

95
00:05:19,087 --> 00:05:22,523
On TV, expert after expert
says the bear market is over.

96
00:05:22,903 --> 00:05:25,530
You still have cash you
haven't invested yet.

97
00:05:25,910 --> 00:05:28,376
Right now, will you decide
the bottom has passed

98
00:05:28,376 --> 00:05:30,585
and invest all your
remaining cash at once?

99
00:05:30,965 --> 00:05:34,889
Or will you wait until the next
price written in your plan arrives?

100
00:05:35,269 --> 00:05:36,209
Make your choice.

101
00:05:36,589 --> 00:05:38,769
3, 2, 1.

102
00:05:39,149 --> 00:05:43,799
Here, the right choice is to wait for
the next price written in your plan.

103
00:05:44,179 --> 00:05:49,779
That day was August 12, 2022, when the
index was up nearly 17% from its June low.

104
00:05:50,159 --> 00:05:53,024
If, like Mr. Jeong, you had
put all your cash in that day,

105
00:05:53,024 --> 00:05:55,790
it would have been down 16%
at the low two months later.

106
00:05:56,170 --> 00:06:01,206
The next price in the plan, 25%
below the high, came on September 30.

107
00:06:01,586 --> 00:06:05,086
That day's price was 16% cheaper
than on the day you went all in.

108
00:06:05,466 --> 00:06:08,656
Just over 3 years later, the
all-in money would have grown 68%,

109
00:06:08,656 --> 00:06:11,035
while the money that
waited would have doubled.

110
00:06:11,415 --> 00:06:13,945
Of course, if that rally
had been the real bottom,

111
00:06:13,945 --> 00:06:16,323
prices might have kept
rising while you waited.

112
00:06:16,703 --> 00:06:21,280
But in a bear market, a rally often
doesn't mean the bottom is in.

113
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:24,028
In bear markets since the dot-com bubble,

114
00:06:24,028 --> 00:06:27,667
six of ten rallies of more than
15% were followed by a new low.

115
00:06:28,047 --> 00:06:31,050
So the right choice is not
to go all in on a rally,

116
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:33,347
but to wait for the next planned price.

117
00:06:34,347 --> 00:06:38,724
A month and a half later, the index
slid again, below its previous low.

118
00:06:39,104 --> 00:06:40,627
It's 24% below the high,

119
00:06:40,627 --> 00:06:44,433
and news that inflation won't
come down stirs up fear again.

120
00:06:44,813 --> 00:06:46,983
Everything you've bought
so far is at a loss.

121
00:06:47,363 --> 00:06:50,595
Forums are flooded with posts saying
buying by the plan is useless.

122
00:06:50,975 --> 00:06:54,603
Right now, will you check that this
money won't affect your daily life,

123
00:06:54,603 --> 00:06:56,034
and keep following the plan?

124
00:06:56,414 --> 00:07:00,371
Or will you decide the plan was wrong,
sell all your index holdings and stop?

125
00:07:00,751 --> 00:07:01,691
Make your choice.

126
00:07:02,071 --> 00:07:04,251
3, 2, 1.

127
00:07:04,631 --> 00:07:08,089
Here, the right choice is to check
your daily life won't be affected,

128
00:07:08,089 --> 00:07:09,492
and keep following the plan.

129
00:07:09,872 --> 00:07:15,184
That day was September 26, 2022, when
the index broke below its June low again.

130
00:07:15,564 --> 00:07:18,265
If, like Mr. Jeong, you had
sold everything and stopped,

131
00:07:18,265 --> 00:07:21,352
interest would have grown your
money only 16% over 3-plus years.

132
00:07:21,732 --> 00:07:27,126
Had you kept to the plan, it would
have dipped 2% more, then risen 96%.

133
00:07:27,506 --> 00:07:31,275
Of course, when the low breaks again,
it feels like the plan itself was wrong.

134
00:07:31,655 --> 00:07:33,922
But the plan was never
about calling the bottom;

135
00:07:33,922 --> 00:07:36,425
it was about buying in parts,
only what you can bear.

136
00:07:36,805 --> 00:07:40,169
In those same bear markets, all
three times the low broke again,

137
00:07:40,169 --> 00:07:41,903
holding was better 4 years later.

138
00:07:42,283 --> 00:07:47,008
If it's money you'll need soon, then
protect your daily life before the plan.

139
00:07:47,388 --> 00:07:50,044
So the right choice: even
if the low breaks again,

140
00:07:50,044 --> 00:07:52,753
if it's spare money, stick
with your original plan.

141
00:07:53,753 --> 00:07:57,063
In this test, how many of your
decisions gave in to fear?

142
00:07:57,443 --> 00:08:02,403
None? You keep buying by the plan with
spare money even in a crash: you pass.

143
00:08:02,783 --> 00:08:08,063
One or two? You have a plan, but fear
or rallies sometimes shake you: caution.

144
00:08:08,443 --> 00:08:10,201
Three or more? Like Mr. Jeong,

145
00:08:10,201 --> 00:08:14,185
you're likely to lose money by
failing to buy out of fear: you fail.

146
00:08:15,185 --> 00:08:17,527
As this test shows, patience pays:

147
00:08:17,527 --> 00:08:21,039
sticking to your plan and
buying through a crash is

148
00:08:21,039 --> 00:08:24,552
the way to get rich with
stocks. Keep that in mind.

149
00:08:24,932 --> 00:08:27,142
I wish you all successful investing.

150
00:08:27,522 --> 00:08:29,052
Thank you for watching.
